{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 4, :comments ["And the sense of <b>\"And I also established My covenant with them\"</b>, <b>\"And I also have heard\"</b>, is: I appeared to them as El Shaddai, and I also established that covenant with them before Me, and now by My great name I too have heard the groan of the children of Israel, and I have remembered My covenant which I established with them, and the enlightened will understand. Now, what our Rabbis expounded concerning this matter (Exodus Rabbah 6:4) — a pity that it is lost and not remembered — is: many times I revealed myself to Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob as El Shaddai, but I did not make known to them that My name is YHWH as I told it to you, yet they did not harbor doubts about My attributes, and so forth, and furthermore they did not ask me what is My name as you asked, and you — at the very beginning of My mission you said to Me, what is Your name, and in the end you said, \"And since I came to Pharaoh\" and so forth (Exodus 5:23), and concerning this it is said, \"And I also established My covenant with them\" and so forth. This midrash too, its meaning is consistent with the verse, for the Sages were troubled: why does He mention the prophecy of the Patriarchs in a way that diminishes their rank in prophecy, saying that He appeared to them only as El Shaddai — what need did He have for this? It would have been fitting to say, \"I am YHWH,\" and therefore, \"Say to the children of Israel: I am YHWH, and I will bring you out, and you shall know that I am YHWH who brings you out.\" Therefore they expounded that the matter was a reproof to Moses, saying to him: behold the Patriarchs, who did not attain your rank in prophecy, and who saw only as El Shaddai — they believed in Me, <b>\"And I also established My covenant with them\"</b>, and I heard the groan of their children for their sake; whereas you, who know Me by the great name and in whom I have instilled trust, such that you have reason to trust in the mercy and to promise Israel by My name that I will perform signs and wonders for them. And this too is correct and fitting:"], :book :exodus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 6, :notes {0 "The Aramaic phrase 'חבל על דאבדין ולא משתכחין' is rendered literally; Ramban cites it as the Sages' own expression of regret over the loss of the full midrashic exposition."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:03:40.758771234Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}